00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:01.290
from the CRE team.

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:03.123
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Sounds
good, let's do that.

00:05:03.123 --> 00:05:07.275
MARK MANDEL: I am super excited
to have the most amazing Luke

00:05:07.275 --> 00:05:08.561
Stone here from the CRE team.

00:05:08.561 --> 00:05:09.810
How are you doing today, Luke?

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:10.950
LUKE STONE: I'm fantastic.

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:12.902
Thanks for the great intro.

00:05:12.902 --> 00:05:13.860
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:16.867
Before we start talking about
CRE and customer reliability

00:05:16.867 --> 00:05:19.450
engineering why don't you tell
us a little bit about yourself.

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:20.491
What do you do at Google?

00:05:20.491 --> 00:05:21.690
What's your role?

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:22.700
Who are you as a person?

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:23.470
LUKE STONE: Sure.

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:24.690
So, my name is Luke Stone.

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:28.410
I am Director of Customer
Reliability Engineering

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:30.770
for the Google Cloud Platform.

00:05:30.770 --> 00:05:36.090
I've been at Google as
an engineer for 14 years.

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:40.440
I started off doing tech
support for AdSense.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:44.130
And I'm really into doing
technical partnerships.

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:46.680
I think Google is an
awesome technical company

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:49.260
and we have a lot
to offer to people

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:51.660
who want to be awesome
at technology too.

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:53.970
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So, we've
done a couple of episodes

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:56.110
where we've mentioned
the concept of CRE,

00:05:56.110 --> 00:05:57.720
but what is it for you?

00:05:57.720 --> 00:05:59.850
LUKE STONE: CRE is
applying the wisdom

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:05.610
and best practices of Google
SRE to our cloud customers.

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:08.360
So SRE is site
reliability engineering.

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:12.034
It's basically Google's
answer to DevOps.

00:06:12.034 --> 00:06:13.950
FRANCESC CAMPOY: We've
got a bunch of episodes

00:06:13.950 --> 00:06:20.470
with Paul Newson and also
other people not Paul Newson.

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:24.180
We talked a little
bit about SRE but SRE

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:27.780
has been there for
quite a while, right?

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:29.910
CRE is pretty new.

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:30.820
When did it start?

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:31.820
How did it start?

00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:36.660
LUKE STONE: CRE started
about six months ago

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:41.910
because we realized that our
current strategies of providing

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:46.080
tech support and
documentation to our customers

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:49.020
was just not sufficient
to help people actually

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:52.180
build meaningful stuff
on our cloud platform.

00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:53.760
So let me take you back.

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:57.450
I joined the cloud
about two years ago.

00:06:57.450 --> 00:07:00.480
At that time we were offering
tech support to our customers,

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:02.370
and this was break fix right.

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:04.830
So something breaks, and
then you open a ticket

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:06.540
and you fix it.

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:11.600
And this is a great service
and absolutely necessary.

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:13.900
It catches everything that
falls through the cracks.

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:18.025
But why do things need to
break in the first place?

00:07:18.025 --> 00:07:19.650
MARK MANDEL: When
you say things break,

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:21.000
do you mean break
on Google Cloud

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:22.250
or break on the customer side?

00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:23.089
Or both?

00:07:23.089 --> 00:07:24.380
LUKE STONE: Well, there you go.

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:26.736
That's the main problem here.

00:07:26.736 --> 00:07:28.110
When you're building
on the cloud

00:07:28.110 --> 00:07:31.420
you get access to a
ton of technology.

00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:33.420
But now your
application is made up

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:37.170
of half of our technology
and half of your technology,

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:40.320
in terms of your application
code and systems.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:44.910
So we really need to
work together and think

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:48.570
of your application as
one technology system

00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:50.220
that we can collaborate on.

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:53.947
And not just a typical
engineering team

00:07:53.947 --> 00:07:56.700
to vendor type relationship.

00:07:56.700 --> 00:07:59.130
Because the APIs are
vast, and we're really

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:00.660
tightly integrated.

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:04.980
What we did two years ago was
try to make our tech support

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:07.920
response times better, and make
the quality of the responses

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:09.210
better.

00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:14.280
We noticed that even the act
of making a tech support ticket

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:17.337
takes several minutes, right?

00:08:17.337 --> 00:08:19.170
And if you're working
on an application that

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:23.100
has a four nines
reliability goal then

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:26.400
you're only allowed
four minutes of downtime

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:28.320
a month so you're gone.

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:29.870
So we really need
to figure out--

00:08:29.870 --> 00:08:33.360
so that wasn't going to
cut it, and we needed

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:35.490
to figure out a new strategy.

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:38.700
So we looked around the world
and said, what's better?

00:08:42.539 --> 00:08:45.360
There's a bunch of stuff
out there in terms of DevOps

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:47.490
and people trying
to do operations

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:52.680
in a more modern style than
the typical developers hate

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:56.400
the operators, and the operators
hate developers, style.

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:58.230
But the one that is
most natural to us

00:08:58.230 --> 00:09:00.690
was Google Site Reliability
Engineering and Google

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:04.320
SRE because all
the teams at Google

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:06.730
operate according
to these principles.

00:09:06.730 --> 00:09:09.210
So I thought, what if we
just apply these principles

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:12.840
to your customer application?

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:13.960
Would it work?

00:09:13.960 --> 00:09:15.940
And so let's give it a shot.

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:18.130
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So what
does that mean exactly?

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:20.220
Does it mean that
whenever someone

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:23.370
wants to build a new
system they're like,

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:26.730
hey, I want to
run these huge web

00:09:26.730 --> 00:09:30.720
servers serving millions of
people and I have an f1-micro.

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:31.850
What's going to happen?

00:09:31.850 --> 00:09:34.530
Or how does it work--
how does it look like?

00:09:34.530 --> 00:09:36.720
LUKE STONE: That's exactly
the kind of scenario

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:37.930
we're trying to address.

00:09:37.930 --> 00:09:41.070
So what we do is we
do the same thing

00:09:41.070 --> 00:09:43.800
that Google SRE does
with Google applications

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:45.332
when they're going
to go production.

00:09:45.332 --> 00:09:47.790
We have this thing that's called
a production and readiness

00:09:47.790 --> 00:09:50.084
review that we do
internally to Google.

00:09:50.084 --> 00:09:51.750
And we have adapted
it to make something

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:54.780
called application reliability
review, for your cloud

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:55.620
application.

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:58.350
So if we were to see
your cloud application

00:09:58.350 --> 00:10:02.860
running on f1-micro we would
say, OK, there's a risk here.

00:10:02.860 --> 00:10:07.594
[LAUGHING]

00:10:07.594 --> 00:10:09.010
People say, well,
what's the risk?

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:12.790
Well, the risk is that your
input traffic is unbounded.

00:10:12.790 --> 00:10:15.627
Do you have any idea of how
much traffic you could get?

00:10:15.627 --> 00:10:16.710
No, I don't have any idea.

00:10:16.710 --> 00:10:20.211
OK, let's do some capacity
planning and modeling

00:10:20.211 --> 00:10:22.210
and see how much traffic
you could possibly get.

00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:23.380
Oh OK, now I have an idea.

00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:26.470
OK, let's do some load testing
and see if that instance is

00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:29.740
going to stand up to it.

00:10:29.740 --> 00:10:30.370
Oh, it's not.

00:10:30.370 --> 00:10:34.487
OK, let's try to develop some
playbook, some procedures--

00:10:34.487 --> 00:10:36.320
MARK MANDEL: Let's have
two micro instances.

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:37.420
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:37.420 --> 00:10:39.190
LUKE STONE: Yeah, exactly.

00:10:39.190 --> 00:10:41.920
And ideally some automation
to help your application

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:43.190
automatically scale.

00:10:43.190 --> 00:10:46.870
But the thing we noticed
is that Google applications

00:10:46.870 --> 00:10:50.030
are designed to scale
from the ground up.

00:10:50.030 --> 00:10:53.650
And we have a lot of
infrastructure and practices

00:10:53.650 --> 00:10:55.840
and history in Google
and building web scale

00:10:55.840 --> 00:10:56.960
applications.

00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:02.230
So the SREs at Google have seen
hundreds of these applications,

00:11:02.230 --> 00:11:06.155
but at most companies
people are working on one.

00:11:06.155 --> 00:11:07.780
And it's their first,
and it's probably

00:11:07.780 --> 00:11:11.410
the only one they're going to
deal with for the next couple

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:12.470
years.

00:11:12.470 --> 00:11:14.950
So we have a lot of
expertise that we

00:11:14.950 --> 00:11:17.040
can share, a lot
of value I think

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:19.510
we can give by just telling
people about what they

00:11:19.510 --> 00:11:21.940
are maybe going to
run into a little bit

00:11:21.940 --> 00:11:22.980
further down the road.

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:24.190
MARK MANDEL: Now,
I'm quite curious,

00:11:24.190 --> 00:11:26.860
obviously without naming names--
and I've seen some blog posts

00:11:26.860 --> 00:11:28.360
on this particularly as well--

00:11:28.360 --> 00:11:30.070
what are particular
lessons that you've

00:11:30.070 --> 00:11:33.370
found working
SRE/CRE for customers

00:11:33.370 --> 00:11:36.430
who are looking to come on board
with Google Cloud Platform?

00:11:36.430 --> 00:11:39.040
What are some common
pitfalls that people kind of

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:41.422
run into on a regular basis?

00:11:41.422 --> 00:11:43.630
LUKE STONE: The best answer
I can really give to that

00:11:43.630 --> 00:11:46.654
is there's this book
called "The SRE Book."

00:11:46.654 --> 00:11:48.820
MARK MANDEL: We might have
mentioned it a few times.

00:11:48.820 --> 00:11:52.630
LUKE STONE: It's basically a
collection of all the stuff

00:11:52.630 --> 00:11:56.830
that SRE has found
over time that

00:11:56.830 --> 00:12:00.190
threatens the reliability
of your application.

00:12:00.190 --> 00:12:02.500
SRE is really a
set of principles

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:06.100
that lead towards an
iterative process of improving

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:08.180
your liability over time.

00:12:08.180 --> 00:12:13.000
So, it kind of works
for almost anything.

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:15.520
The core principles,
just to say them again,

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:18.380
are know what your SLOs are.

00:12:18.380 --> 00:12:21.490
And SLO is a service
level objective.

00:12:21.490 --> 00:12:24.070
So it's basically what
is your expected uptime,

00:12:24.070 --> 00:12:26.230
or what is your desired up time.

00:12:26.230 --> 00:12:29.000
And then have a consequence
for missing that,

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:30.760
and we call that
an error budget.

00:12:30.760 --> 00:12:33.160
So at Google a
typical consequence

00:12:33.160 --> 00:12:39.940
is if we don't meet our SLO then
we stop pushing new features.

00:12:39.940 --> 00:12:41.650
And we use the
development time instead

00:12:41.650 --> 00:12:45.730
to improve the
reliability of our system.

00:12:45.730 --> 00:12:49.180
If you start with that it
starts to align incentives,

00:12:49.180 --> 00:12:50.830
and then there's a
lot more incentives

00:12:50.830 --> 00:12:54.040
you need to align between the
developers and the operators

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:56.260
and the system owners.

00:12:56.260 --> 00:12:59.740
Until you can get to a state
where every time something bad

00:12:59.740 --> 00:13:03.370
happens the response
is a positive response

00:13:03.370 --> 00:13:06.190
that ensures that it
won't happen again.

00:13:06.190 --> 00:13:09.010
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So I'm
curious about how that exactly

00:13:09.010 --> 00:13:12.190
works when you are on CRE.

00:13:12.190 --> 00:13:15.610
Because do you actually
tell the customer, oh no,

00:13:15.610 --> 00:13:17.860
you cannot push
any more features.

00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:18.736
How does that happen?

00:13:18.736 --> 00:13:20.235
LUKE STONE: That's
a great question.

00:13:20.235 --> 00:13:22.190
So the very basic way
that it works in SRE

00:13:22.190 --> 00:13:24.670
is you have a post-mortem.

00:13:24.670 --> 00:13:26.690
So if something
goes wrong, and then

00:13:26.690 --> 00:13:30.970
you do a post-mortem,
which, there

00:13:30.970 --> 00:13:32.470
is a very structured
approach to it.

00:13:32.470 --> 00:13:34.420
You come over the
timeline of events

00:13:34.420 --> 00:13:37.050
and you establish
some basic facts

00:13:37.050 --> 00:13:38.770
so everybody is in agreement.

00:13:38.770 --> 00:13:41.320
We use a blameless
post-modern culture,

00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:49.300
so if you broke something it's
great that you raise your hand

00:13:49.300 --> 00:13:52.000
and tell people what
you did exactly.

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:54.970
So that we can learn how
to avoid it in the future.

00:13:54.970 --> 00:13:57.730
So we do blameless post-mortems.

00:13:57.730 --> 00:14:00.790
And then we go through what
went wrong, what went well,

00:14:00.790 --> 00:14:03.790
and what we're going to do to
prevent it happening again.

00:14:03.790 --> 00:14:07.300
And it basically turns into
a list of action items.

00:14:07.300 --> 00:14:11.170
You can see how this will
work to eventually squeeze

00:14:11.170 --> 00:14:14.180
reliability risks
out of the system.

00:14:14.180 --> 00:14:16.510
But in the very
beginning you don't

00:14:16.510 --> 00:14:19.270
want to just start waiting for
things to go wrong and then fix

00:14:19.270 --> 00:14:19.990
them.

00:14:19.990 --> 00:14:24.560
So we can leapfrog ahead and do
an application or reliability

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:25.190
review.

00:14:25.190 --> 00:14:27.390
We sit down and we look
at your application

00:14:27.390 --> 00:14:30.000
and we use our expertise about
what has broken in the past

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:31.541
to try to guess
what's going to break

00:14:31.541 --> 00:14:34.030
with your application
in the future.

00:14:34.030 --> 00:14:38.440
And we go through five or six
components of your application

00:14:38.440 --> 00:14:41.860
and try to identify what
are the biggest risks

00:14:41.860 --> 00:14:43.480
in each one of these things.

00:14:43.480 --> 00:14:49.300
We'll color them red, orange,
green, in terms of risk level.

00:14:49.300 --> 00:14:53.414
We think about it in terms
of the impact of the problem.

00:14:53.414 --> 00:14:55.330
Is it going to take your
site completely down?

00:14:55.330 --> 00:14:56.860
Or is going to make it slow?

00:14:56.860 --> 00:14:59.980
Or is it just going to
be an inconvenience?

00:14:59.980 --> 00:15:02.440
And then the frequency.

00:15:02.440 --> 00:15:04.440
Is it likely to
happen once a day?

00:15:04.440 --> 00:15:05.380
Or once a month?

00:15:05.380 --> 00:15:07.276
Or once every couple years?

00:15:07.276 --> 00:15:08.650
So we can make a
matrix and think

00:15:08.650 --> 00:15:12.982
about what's the potential
impact of each risk.

00:15:12.982 --> 00:15:14.690
And then we'll give
you a report and say,

00:15:14.690 --> 00:15:16.690
this is what we
think is the risk

00:15:16.690 --> 00:15:18.230
profile of your application.

00:15:18.230 --> 00:15:20.219
They might have two
dozen things in it.

00:15:20.219 --> 00:15:21.760
So for each one of
these things we'll

00:15:21.760 --> 00:15:26.140
say here's maybe what
we would suggest you do.

00:15:26.140 --> 00:15:29.242
But then, really, you're in
charge of your engineering team

00:15:29.242 --> 00:15:31.450
as the customer and you're
building this application.

00:15:31.450 --> 00:15:33.145
So, you can take
this information

00:15:33.145 --> 00:15:35.030
and you can take it or leave it.

00:15:35.030 --> 00:15:36.730
And we fully understand that.

00:15:36.730 --> 00:15:41.830
So if you want to fix all those
things, that's super great

00:15:41.830 --> 00:15:44.750
and we will help you do it.

00:15:44.750 --> 00:15:46.750
And we will advise you
on the best way to do it,

00:15:46.750 --> 00:15:49.570
and will help you connect with
the right people at Google

00:15:49.570 --> 00:15:54.522
for feature requests
or product gaps.

00:15:54.522 --> 00:15:56.230
We'll also take action
items on that list

00:15:56.230 --> 00:15:58.810
as well for ourselves.

00:15:58.810 --> 00:16:00.820
To build things that
will make it easier

00:16:00.820 --> 00:16:02.410
to support your application.

00:16:02.410 --> 00:16:05.710
Like for example,
if your application

00:16:05.710 --> 00:16:10.120
uses a database that is
subject to some hotspotting

00:16:10.120 --> 00:16:13.090
because a sharded
database like a data

00:16:13.090 --> 00:16:18.070
store or a Memcache service, we
would consider building tools

00:16:18.070 --> 00:16:21.135
to identify hotspotting.

00:16:21.135 --> 00:16:22.760
To make your application
more reliable.

00:16:22.760 --> 00:16:25.540
So we both take
actions off this list.

00:16:25.540 --> 00:16:29.830
Then the deal is, if we can get
all the critical action items

00:16:29.830 --> 00:16:32.990
off the list then we can
kind of go into a next phase

00:16:32.990 --> 00:16:37.570
where we can operate it together
with some shared context.

00:16:37.570 --> 00:16:40.750
So now we're pretty
sure that it's not going

00:16:40.750 --> 00:16:43.570
to crash for no reason at all.

00:16:43.570 --> 00:16:45.310
We're pretty sure
we squeezed out most

00:16:45.310 --> 00:16:48.400
of the predictable reasons.

00:16:48.400 --> 00:16:52.620
Let's agree that if we're
faced with an outage situation

00:16:52.620 --> 00:16:55.840
that we will jump on a bridge
together and start debugging it

00:16:55.840 --> 00:16:56.510
together.

00:16:56.510 --> 00:17:00.460
That's kind of what we call
shared operational fate, which

00:17:00.460 --> 00:17:06.069
is really the end state
of a CRE engagement.

00:17:06.069 --> 00:17:08.140
That's the state
that we would love

00:17:08.140 --> 00:17:10.569
to get to because it's this
state that most resembles

00:17:10.569 --> 00:17:14.290
the way that Google
SRE teams work together

00:17:14.290 --> 00:17:15.516
to keep an application going.

00:17:15.516 --> 00:17:16.849
MARK MANDEL: So it sounds like--

00:17:16.849 --> 00:17:19.690
OK, so for CRE it sounds like
the difference between CRE

00:17:19.690 --> 00:17:22.443
and SRE is that you seem to
be doing more up front work

00:17:22.443 --> 00:17:23.609
than you would do in an SRE.

00:17:23.609 --> 00:17:24.579
Is that correct?

00:17:24.579 --> 00:17:27.319
LUKE STONE: I would say
it's about the same.

00:17:27.319 --> 00:17:32.230
I mean, it's very interesting
because the SRE team at Google

00:17:32.230 --> 00:17:36.700
does not support
all Google products.

00:17:36.700 --> 00:17:38.680
One of the principles
is scarcity,

00:17:38.680 --> 00:17:40.300
so you make sure
that you support

00:17:40.300 --> 00:17:42.360
the most important ones.

00:17:42.360 --> 00:17:43.930
And then you give
people an incentive

00:17:43.930 --> 00:17:47.380
to work on their own reliability
so they can get to a point

00:17:47.380 --> 00:17:51.940
where it's fair to engage
with another operational team.

00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:55.750
Because you've done
your homework and you've

00:17:55.750 --> 00:17:57.760
done all the basic things.

00:17:57.760 --> 00:18:00.040
So in Google SRE,
usually the way

00:18:00.040 --> 00:18:02.770
you get access to
an SRE team is you

00:18:02.770 --> 00:18:04.880
go through a readiness review.

00:18:04.880 --> 00:18:10.420
And then you polish off
all the known problems.

00:18:10.420 --> 00:18:14.560
And often it takes many months,
like, a year or 18 months

00:18:14.560 --> 00:18:18.310
to really build trust
with the SRE team

00:18:18.310 --> 00:18:22.180
that your development team
is thinking about reliability

00:18:22.180 --> 00:18:26.800
enough to invest SRE
resources into it as well.

00:18:26.800 --> 00:18:31.120
In that regard I think we're a
little faster to market in CRE

00:18:31.120 --> 00:18:33.580
but we try to use
the same principles.

00:18:33.580 --> 00:18:35.530
MARK MANDEL: OK cool,
so there's actually

00:18:35.530 --> 00:18:38.062
quite a lot of similarity
between SRE and CRE.

00:18:38.062 --> 00:18:40.270
Really it's just the end
user more than anything else

00:18:40.270 --> 00:18:42.850
LUKE STONE: Yeah, and
my job is really--

00:18:42.850 --> 00:18:46.930
so my job within the CRE team is
I'm the customer lead for CRE.

00:18:46.930 --> 00:18:50.120
So what I do is, I go through
all the practices of SRE

00:18:50.120 --> 00:18:54.160
and I try to figure out which
ones actually apply only

00:18:54.160 --> 00:18:58.210
to Google, and which ones
are possible to generalize

00:18:58.210 --> 00:18:59.680
to all the customer base.

00:18:59.680 --> 00:19:04.540
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So to get ready
to get SRE support at Google

00:19:04.540 --> 00:19:09.214
I'm imagining that before
you get the readiness review

00:19:09.214 --> 00:19:10.630
there's some kind
of documentation

00:19:10.630 --> 00:19:13.540
that the teams can go
through and basically

00:19:13.540 --> 00:19:16.900
pre evaluate themselves before
they go to the actual review.

00:19:16.900 --> 00:19:20.080
Is there such a thing
also externally?

00:19:20.080 --> 00:19:22.390
Like if someone or
some of our customers

00:19:22.390 --> 00:19:24.940
are like, oh, am I good enough?

00:19:24.940 --> 00:19:28.720
What are the best practices
that they should be following?

00:19:28.720 --> 00:19:31.850
LUKE STONE: The thing
to start with is SLOs.

00:19:31.850 --> 00:19:34.150
That's really the
tip of the spear.

00:19:34.150 --> 00:19:37.120
The SLO is really your
contract with the rest

00:19:37.120 --> 00:19:40.330
of your organization
about how reliable

00:19:40.330 --> 00:19:43.160
you expect your
application to be.

00:19:43.160 --> 00:19:46.190
And it's easy to say, but it's
kind of hard to think about.

00:19:46.190 --> 00:19:50.650
So imagine in a case where
your application goes down

00:19:50.650 --> 00:19:56.170
for one minute, but your
CEO happens to be using it

00:19:56.170 --> 00:19:57.590
right at that minute.

00:19:57.590 --> 00:20:01.000
They're going to page the ops
team and scream real loud.

00:20:01.000 --> 00:20:09.620
But in reality if you have an
SLO that's below five nines

00:20:09.620 --> 00:20:10.620
then that's probably OK.

00:20:10.620 --> 00:20:12.900
It's within the expected bounds.

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:15.780
You need to have an agreement
across your organization

00:20:15.780 --> 00:20:19.530
that you're not going to
drop everything and focus

00:20:19.530 --> 00:20:23.730
on a problem if
it's within bounds.

00:20:23.730 --> 00:20:27.051
And on the other hand, if
the problem is out of bounds

00:20:27.051 --> 00:20:28.800
then you need to be
able to say, OK, we're

00:20:28.800 --> 00:20:30.341
going to have a
consequence and we're

00:20:30.341 --> 00:20:33.130
going to change our
behavior in a known way

00:20:33.130 --> 00:20:34.440
when we go out of bounds.

00:20:34.440 --> 00:20:39.060
So it's a lot easier to
prenegotiate this stuff,

00:20:39.060 --> 00:20:41.100
because it takes a
while to negotiate.

00:20:41.100 --> 00:20:46.380
It has a lot to do with
headcount and product release,

00:20:46.380 --> 00:20:52.177
feature velocity, and when
things ship and go to market.

00:20:52.177 --> 00:20:53.760
So it's going to be
a little different

00:20:53.760 --> 00:20:56.110
in every company I think.

00:20:56.110 --> 00:20:58.560
But at Google we use this
concept of error budget, which

00:20:58.560 --> 00:21:01.500
basically says if
your reliability is

00:21:01.500 --> 00:21:03.750
below your target and
it looks like you're

00:21:03.750 --> 00:21:08.520
going to miss for the
month, you stop releasing.

00:21:08.520 --> 00:21:12.120
That helps to reallocate
your development effort

00:21:12.120 --> 00:21:15.210
towards reliability,
bugs, and stuff.

00:21:15.210 --> 00:21:18.540
I mean, everybody has
a reliability backlog.

00:21:18.540 --> 00:21:22.650
It's called P3 in
your bug queue.

00:21:22.650 --> 00:21:24.660
MARK MANDEL: Does that
mean the CRE team will

00:21:24.660 --> 00:21:27.900
share SLOs or possibly
SLAs with the customer

00:21:27.900 --> 00:21:30.020
once they're engaged,
is that how that works?

00:21:30.020 --> 00:21:32.160
So it's kind of like a
group mentality, I guess?

00:21:32.160 --> 00:21:33.201
LUKE STONE: That's right.

00:21:33.201 --> 00:21:36.570
So what we're trying to do
is distill down the interface

00:21:36.570 --> 00:21:38.160
between our two subsystems.

00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:40.730
We're running the infrastructure
system, basically,

00:21:40.730 --> 00:21:43.530
the cloud computing platform.

00:21:43.530 --> 00:21:45.250
And you're running
your application.

00:21:45.250 --> 00:21:47.010
And so we want to
figure out what's

00:21:47.010 --> 00:21:52.740
a really good contract between
our two groups about when

00:21:52.740 --> 00:21:54.040
we alert each other.

00:21:54.040 --> 00:21:56.270
So we want to try to think
about your application

00:21:56.270 --> 00:21:58.530
as being somewhat
of a black box.

00:21:58.530 --> 00:22:01.650
Usually we own a bunch of
networking and infrastructure

00:22:01.650 --> 00:22:03.630
at the front end--

00:22:03.630 --> 00:22:06.167
load balancing, external
networking and stuff--

00:22:06.167 --> 00:22:07.750
and then we have
your application code

00:22:07.750 --> 00:22:09.370
which is in the middle.

00:22:09.370 --> 00:22:12.120
And then we have a lot
of services like storage,

00:22:12.120 --> 00:22:14.830
or PUB/SUB, or something
on the back end.

00:22:14.830 --> 00:22:18.570
So we try to think about what
would be the indicators that we

00:22:18.570 --> 00:22:21.150
could both observe,
at the same time,

00:22:21.150 --> 00:22:23.190
at those boundaries
that will tell us

00:22:23.190 --> 00:22:25.360
that the applications
is not performing well.

00:22:25.360 --> 00:22:28.880
And if we can agree on
a few of those things,

00:22:28.880 --> 00:22:32.580
let's say, we'll look
at the load balancer

00:22:32.580 --> 00:22:34.800
error rate and latency.

00:22:34.800 --> 00:22:37.870
We can make a graph
of that metric

00:22:37.870 --> 00:22:40.560
and we can put it in a place
where both of our teams

00:22:40.560 --> 00:22:41.670
can see it.

00:22:41.670 --> 00:22:44.250
So now we have what
we call a shared state

00:22:44.250 --> 00:22:45.870
about what's going on.

00:22:45.870 --> 00:22:49.050
And if someone has a concern
about something going wrong

00:22:49.050 --> 00:22:51.300
we can both look at that
graph and see the same thing.

00:22:51.300 --> 00:22:52.716
We can say, oh
yeah wait a minute,

00:22:52.716 --> 00:22:55.400
errors are way, way, out
of bounds, let's act.

00:22:55.400 --> 00:22:58.380
Or we can say, hey look,
errors are actually in bounds

00:22:58.380 --> 00:22:59.760
let's hold off for a second.

00:22:59.760 --> 00:23:04.050
We're not going to hit the red
button and page of the world.

00:23:04.050 --> 00:23:08.910
So it helps a lot to get
into a rhythm of knowing

00:23:08.910 --> 00:23:12.230
what to expect from the other
team's behavior over time.

00:23:12.230 --> 00:23:14.940
And it helps to build trust
and helps to build reliability.

00:23:14.940 --> 00:23:16.690
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
we talked a little bit

00:23:16.690 --> 00:23:21.150
about what happens when you
are about to miss your SLO.

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:24.180
What about when you
are way above your SLO?

00:23:24.180 --> 00:23:27.960
Do we do something with
that extra error budget?

00:23:27.960 --> 00:23:29.460
Or does that never happen?

00:23:29.460 --> 00:23:31.980
LUKE STONE: That's
a great question.

00:23:31.980 --> 00:23:38.370
What we see a lot, is people
are being really conservative

00:23:38.370 --> 00:23:40.420
about their applications.

00:23:40.420 --> 00:23:44.160
At Google, one thing we've
done that's very impressive

00:23:44.160 --> 00:23:47.640
is to focus on our
feature velocity

00:23:47.640 --> 00:23:49.440
and how fast we can ship things.

00:23:49.440 --> 00:23:52.470
And to be thoughtful
about trading off

00:23:52.470 --> 00:23:56.100
how fast we can ship versus
how reliable we're going to be.

00:23:56.100 --> 00:24:01.080
So, often I see people erring on
the side of being too cautious.

00:24:01.080 --> 00:24:04.110
They want to have only five
minutes of downtime of year

00:24:04.110 --> 00:24:08.085
when it turns out
the internet has

00:24:08.085 --> 00:24:09.960
an hour or a half of
downtime and if you have

00:24:09.960 --> 00:24:13.350
a mobile application
you might be

00:24:13.350 --> 00:24:17.820
unconnected from your
application for many hours,

00:24:17.820 --> 00:24:19.120
or even days a year.

00:24:19.120 --> 00:24:20.910
People over optimize
for reliability

00:24:20.910 --> 00:24:25.240
and miss the chance to ship
features and get good velocity.

00:24:25.240 --> 00:24:28.050
So what we're trying
to provide is, really,

00:24:28.050 --> 00:24:31.540
a way to manage your reliability
according to your business

00:24:31.540 --> 00:24:32.070
goals.

00:24:32.070 --> 00:24:35.760
MARK MANDEL: So I was reading on
the announcement blog post that

00:24:35.760 --> 00:24:38.310
was talking about CRE
and everything like that

00:24:38.310 --> 00:24:41.763
and it said on there,
additional cost of zero dollars.

00:24:41.763 --> 00:24:42.729
What?

00:24:42.729 --> 00:24:44.180
[LAUGHING]

00:24:44.180 --> 00:24:49.060
LUKE STONE: Well, that does two
things that are very important.

00:24:49.060 --> 00:24:52.230
First of all it
sets the expectation

00:24:52.230 --> 00:24:54.700
that we're going to
have to work together.

00:24:54.700 --> 00:24:58.980
So it doesn't actually
make it easier

00:24:58.980 --> 00:25:02.250
to run your application
reliably if you give me

00:25:02.250 --> 00:25:03.660
a suitcase of money.

00:25:03.660 --> 00:25:06.180
That doesn't actually help.

00:25:06.180 --> 00:25:10.020
The problems are often in
the design of your service.

00:25:10.020 --> 00:25:19.110
And does it have any features
to auto scale or to drop traffic

00:25:19.110 --> 00:25:20.340
when it's overloaded?

00:25:20.340 --> 00:25:24.090
Or to reshard when
it has a hot shard?

00:25:24.090 --> 00:25:27.120
I can't really solve those
with a suitcase full of money.

00:25:27.120 --> 00:25:29.910
So what I really need
from you is I need time

00:25:29.910 --> 00:25:31.560
with your engineering team.

00:25:31.560 --> 00:25:33.750
So the additional cost
is actually zero dollars

00:25:33.750 --> 00:25:37.590
but it's fairly heavy in terms
of engineering investment

00:25:37.590 --> 00:25:42.420
I want to work with the
tech leads of your services

00:25:42.420 --> 00:25:46.170
and be able to manage
to do less with them,

00:25:46.170 --> 00:25:48.950
meet with them regularly, and
be in constant communication.

00:25:48.950 --> 00:25:50.408
Because that's
really what it takes

00:25:50.408 --> 00:25:51.810
to operate things reliably.

00:25:51.810 --> 00:25:55.770
The second thing it does
to offer zero dollars

00:25:55.770 --> 00:25:59.400
additional cost is it
gives us the ability

00:25:59.400 --> 00:26:06.090
to hand back the pagers if
the service is too unreliable

00:26:06.090 --> 00:26:07.590
and if you are not
doing anything--

00:26:07.590 --> 00:26:09.630
if you're not,
basically, holding up

00:26:09.630 --> 00:26:11.390
your end of the bargain.

00:26:11.390 --> 00:26:15.150
In Google we have
this concept as well.

00:26:15.150 --> 00:26:18.820
It's not because it's intended
to be executed frequently.

00:26:18.820 --> 00:26:20.700
All we need to do
is show that this

00:26:20.700 --> 00:26:23.024
is a dynamic that can play out.

00:26:23.024 --> 00:26:24.690
And that gives people
a strong incentive

00:26:24.690 --> 00:26:27.690
to actually carry their
end of the bargain.

00:26:27.690 --> 00:26:29.670
Because really what we're
trying to do here is

00:26:29.670 --> 00:26:30.770
create a partnership.

00:26:30.770 --> 00:26:33.300
MARK MANDEL: So if you show up
with a the list of 12 things

00:26:33.300 --> 00:26:35.790
and they're like, no
we're not gonna do that.

00:26:35.790 --> 00:26:39.090
Then you're like, OK, then we're
not going to do our bit either.

00:26:39.090 --> 00:26:40.350
LUKE STONE: Right, exactly.

00:26:40.350 --> 00:26:45.450
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So we kept
on saying the word customer,

00:26:45.450 --> 00:26:47.250
and we've interviewed
some of them that

00:26:47.250 --> 00:26:49.740
are some of your CRE customers.

00:26:49.740 --> 00:26:53.790
We talked recently, in episode
68, with the Home Depot.

00:26:53.790 --> 00:26:55.910
THE Home Depot.

00:26:55.910 --> 00:26:57.380
Also we interviewed
at some point

00:26:57.380 --> 00:27:00.750
Edward Wu from Niantic
that built Pokemon Go.

00:27:00.750 --> 00:27:03.240
What other customers
can you tell us about?

00:27:03.240 --> 00:27:06.661
LUKE STONE: One that's very
interesting is Pivotal.

00:27:06.661 --> 00:27:08.910
We've been working with
Pivotal on their Cloud Foundry

00:27:08.910 --> 00:27:13.050
product, which is basically
a way to build applications

00:27:13.050 --> 00:27:15.710
that can run on a cloud--

00:27:15.710 --> 00:27:18.261
a private cloud
or a public cloud.

00:27:18.261 --> 00:27:20.010
With a lot of services
for the developers.

00:27:20.010 --> 00:27:22.740
The developer doesn't
have to really think about

00:27:22.740 --> 00:27:25.310
where is their going to run,
or what services can it access.

00:27:25.310 --> 00:27:28.860
Their concept is, here's my
code run it on the cloud,

00:27:28.860 --> 00:27:30.780
I don't care how.

00:27:30.780 --> 00:27:34.074
So that works really great
because we have a cloud.

00:27:34.074 --> 00:27:35.490
They have a lot
of software that's

00:27:35.490 --> 00:27:37.406
kind of in between the
developer and the cloud

00:27:37.406 --> 00:27:39.904
and we're helping them to
understand how that software is

00:27:39.904 --> 00:27:41.820
going to scale, how it's
going to be reliable,

00:27:41.820 --> 00:27:43.680
how to monitor it on our cloud.

00:27:43.680 --> 00:27:47.340
They're working down a list
of bugs right now of ways

00:27:47.340 --> 00:27:50.610
to make it more reliable and
I think it's going to be--

00:27:50.610 --> 00:27:53.550
the nice way that
impacts the marketplace

00:27:53.550 --> 00:27:55.710
is that it really
scales our effort.

00:27:55.710 --> 00:28:00.900
We can't talk to every
single customer one on one.

00:28:00.900 --> 00:28:03.510
But if we work with Pivotal
to make their Cloud Foundry

00:28:03.510 --> 00:28:06.660
software really reliable
then lots of customers

00:28:06.660 --> 00:28:08.450
can benefit from that.

00:28:08.450 --> 00:28:10.772
And we don't have to
explain the same concepts

00:28:10.772 --> 00:28:11.980
over and over and over again.

00:28:11.980 --> 00:28:13.410
MARK MANDEL: So we're getting
a little bit close to time,

00:28:13.410 --> 00:28:14.250
but that's OK.

00:28:14.250 --> 00:28:15.960
So, plans for the
future for CRE?

00:28:15.960 --> 00:28:17.520
Where do you see
this thing heading?

00:28:17.520 --> 00:28:22.530
LUKE STONE: I'd like to work
with our biggest customers

00:28:22.530 --> 00:28:23.770
first.

00:28:23.770 --> 00:28:25.980
The people who can really
invest the most engineering

00:28:25.980 --> 00:28:28.140
effort with us.

00:28:28.140 --> 00:28:29.690
Because I think
we'll learn a lot

00:28:29.690 --> 00:28:32.460
that can be spread
out to more customers.

00:28:32.460 --> 00:28:38.250
Over time, of course, SRE
values software and automation

00:28:38.250 --> 00:28:40.240
as a primary technique
for getting things done.

00:28:40.240 --> 00:28:42.850
We're going to use our first
engagements that are really

00:28:42.850 --> 00:28:47.220
person to person to learn
use cases and failure modes.

00:28:47.220 --> 00:28:50.070
And then we're going
to develop tools that

00:28:50.070 --> 00:28:51.840
will avoid this in the future.

00:28:51.840 --> 00:28:55.140
My plan is to work with,
like, an order of tens

00:28:55.140 --> 00:28:56.910
of customers in the first year.

00:28:56.910 --> 00:29:00.190
And then an order of hundreds
the next year, thousands

00:29:00.190 --> 00:29:01.480
the year after that.

00:29:01.480 --> 00:29:07.149
So we'll get to the point
where we have some impact

00:29:07.149 --> 00:29:08.940
and you're getting some
value from our team

00:29:08.940 --> 00:29:11.820
even if you're not
talking to us directly.

00:29:11.820 --> 00:29:15.510
We plan to-- we'll have
features show up in Google Cloud

00:29:15.510 --> 00:29:17.269
that are reliability features.

00:29:17.269 --> 00:29:19.560
MARK MANDEL: All right, well
we are running out of time

00:29:19.560 --> 00:29:19.860
unfortunately.

00:29:19.860 --> 00:29:21.990
I'd love to talk to you
for hours and hours on end,

00:29:21.990 --> 00:29:24.735
but is there anything we haven't
mentioned you want to plug

00:29:24.735 --> 00:29:27.360
or that you just think is super,
super cool, that people should

00:29:27.360 --> 00:29:29.670
hear about that we haven't
had a chance to talk about.

00:29:29.670 --> 00:29:33.940
LUKE STONE: One thing is
that SRE is, as a concept,

00:29:33.940 --> 00:29:38.790
is getting a lot of traction
in the DevOps community.

00:29:38.790 --> 00:29:42.550
And we just had SRECon here in
San Francisco, it was awesome.

00:29:42.550 --> 00:29:46.080
There were hundreds of people
here who were really into it

00:29:46.080 --> 00:29:48.630
and they were all
adding a lot of value

00:29:48.630 --> 00:29:50.340
and sharing a lot of ideas.

00:29:50.340 --> 00:29:53.340
So we want to be a conduit
of that kind of information

00:29:53.340 --> 00:29:55.950
out to the world as well.

00:29:55.950 --> 00:30:00.210
And we'll be at Google
Cloud Next in New York

00:30:00.210 --> 00:30:03.930
City and Seattle coming up with
dates available on the Google

00:30:03.930 --> 00:30:05.070
Cloud Next website.

00:30:05.070 --> 00:30:06.028
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Great!

00:30:06.028 --> 00:30:08.160
We'll have links
on the show notes.

00:30:08.160 --> 00:30:09.570
I guess it's time
to say goodbye,

00:30:09.570 --> 00:30:13.260
thank you so much for coming
and spending this time with us

00:30:13.260 --> 00:30:15.750
and letting us know
so much about CRE.

00:30:15.750 --> 00:30:18.390
Hopefully looking forward
to having you again sometime

00:30:18.390 --> 00:30:20.680
soon to tell us about how
is it growing into hundreds

00:30:20.680 --> 00:30:23.480
and thousands of customers.

00:30:23.480 --> 00:30:25.860
LUKE STONE: We'll
see you next year.

00:30:25.860 --> 00:30:27.330
Thanks, Mark and Francesc.

00:30:27.330 --> 00:30:28.955
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Thanks so much to Luke

00:30:28.955 --> 00:30:30.720
for such an
interesting interview.

00:30:30.720 --> 00:30:35.370
I've learned so much about how
we interact with customers when

00:30:35.370 --> 00:30:36.990
we're building big stuff.

00:30:36.990 --> 00:30:37.800
Very, very cool.

00:30:37.800 --> 00:30:40.920
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, super happy
to have Luke on the podcast.

00:30:40.920 --> 00:30:42.750
It's really interesting
to hear about how

00:30:42.750 --> 00:30:44.680
we are engaging with customers.

00:30:44.680 --> 00:30:46.890
Especially in enterprise
level on the sort of support

00:30:46.890 --> 00:30:49.170
we're able to give them, which
makes me super happy to see.

00:30:49.170 --> 00:30:50.753
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, it's basically,

00:30:50.753 --> 00:30:54.480
they're not our customers they
are our partners on building

00:30:54.480 --> 00:30:55.050
cool stuff.

00:30:55.050 --> 00:30:55.820
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, I like that.

00:30:55.820 --> 00:30:57.310
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So, it is
that different approach,

00:30:57.310 --> 00:30:58.140
I really like it.

00:30:58.140 --> 00:31:02.010
So I think it's time to go
to our question of the week.

00:31:02.010 --> 00:31:06.360
Today, the question of
the week comes from again,

00:31:06.360 --> 00:31:08.380
Rokesh, Rokesh Jankie.

00:31:08.380 --> 00:31:11.730
We answered one of his
questions on episode 70

00:31:11.730 --> 00:31:13.800
about protocol buffers.

00:31:13.800 --> 00:31:16.830
As soon as I sent him
an email saying, hey,

00:31:16.830 --> 00:31:19.380
your question has been
answered he was like, well,

00:31:19.380 --> 00:31:20.830
I have another question.

00:31:20.830 --> 00:31:23.300
So we are going to be
answering that question now.

00:31:23.300 --> 00:31:29.220
The question is, many
places they give you an SLA.

00:31:29.220 --> 00:31:35.090
They give you, oh, we guarantee
99.99% percent of availability.

00:31:35.090 --> 00:31:36.570
But why not 100?

00:31:36.570 --> 00:31:37.740
Could they do it?

00:31:37.740 --> 00:31:40.510
MARK MANDEL: In theory, you
could have 100% availability

00:31:40.510 --> 00:31:42.240
if you never changed any code.

00:31:42.240 --> 00:31:42.977
In theory.

00:31:42.977 --> 00:31:45.060
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I, actually,
do not believe that.

00:31:45.060 --> 00:31:46.120
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
that is also true.

00:31:46.120 --> 00:31:48.690
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Even if
you don't change any code,

00:31:48.690 --> 00:31:51.000
if your system is available
through the internet

00:31:51.000 --> 00:31:53.649
the internet is
not available 100%.

00:31:53.649 --> 00:31:54.690
MARK MANDEL: That's true.

00:31:54.690 --> 00:31:56.532
So, like I said,
in theory you could

00:31:56.532 --> 00:31:58.990
but there's lots of stuff that
can go wrong in the process.

00:31:58.990 --> 00:32:00.698
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
the interesting thing

00:32:00.698 --> 00:32:05.280
is that you could have a 100%
SLA, Service Level Agreement,

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:08.400
which means that if
your service does not

00:32:08.400 --> 00:32:12.510
offer 100% availability it
means that they will somehow

00:32:12.510 --> 00:32:13.990
compensate for it.

00:32:13.990 --> 00:32:14.970
That is an SLA.

00:32:14.970 --> 00:32:15.510
MARK MANDEL: That's true.

00:32:15.510 --> 00:32:16.560
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Which
doesn't mean they

00:32:16.560 --> 00:32:18.180
will have 100% availability.

00:32:18.180 --> 00:32:20.374
Because that is
literally impossible.

00:32:20.374 --> 00:32:22.290
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, as
you said stuff goes down

00:32:22.290 --> 00:32:22.998
and things break.

00:32:22.998 --> 00:32:26.250
But I think, also, it's
a good point that you're

00:32:26.250 --> 00:32:28.830
going to want new features
to come into your application

00:32:28.830 --> 00:32:30.960
or whatever it is that
you have to be building.

00:32:30.960 --> 00:32:33.289
And any change you make is risk.

00:32:33.289 --> 00:32:35.330
And there's nothing you
can really do about that.

00:32:35.330 --> 00:32:37.510
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
we talked about this.

00:32:37.510 --> 00:32:41.490
With Paul Newson, our
dear Paul Newson, on 59.

00:32:41.490 --> 00:32:44.400
So if you're interested
in knowing more of this

00:32:44.400 --> 00:32:46.530
that's definitely an
episode that you might

00:32:46.530 --> 00:32:48.120
be interested in listening to.

00:32:48.120 --> 00:32:50.160
But I think that the
most interesting thing

00:32:50.160 --> 00:32:53.880
is what you were mentioning
before about what is actually

00:32:53.880 --> 00:32:56.650
five nines, or six nines,
or what-- to start with,

00:32:56.650 --> 00:33:00.150
five nines, does that mean
nine nine, and five more nines?

00:33:00.150 --> 00:33:02.910
Or nine nine, dot,
and three nines?

00:33:02.910 --> 00:33:04.210
Because it is ambiguous.

00:33:04.210 --> 00:33:07.380
MARK MANDEL: Five
nines, 99.999%.

00:33:07.380 --> 00:33:09.913
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK, so
the two nines in the 99%

00:33:09.913 --> 00:33:10.747
are already counted.

00:33:10.747 --> 00:33:12.038
MARK MANDEL: They're all nines.

00:33:12.038 --> 00:33:13.320
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Good, OK.

00:33:13.320 --> 00:33:14.275
You never know.

00:33:14.275 --> 00:33:15.900
MARK MANDEL: This
goes to a good point,

00:33:15.900 --> 00:33:17.490
and Rokesh alluded
to in his question

00:33:17.490 --> 00:33:21.450
when he was like, what does it
mean that Spanner isn't 100%.

00:33:21.450 --> 00:33:23.420
Spanner has five
nines availability.

00:33:23.420 --> 00:33:25.920
So 99.999% availability.

00:33:25.920 --> 00:33:28.530
What does that mean for
mission critical applications?

00:33:28.530 --> 00:33:30.651
So you sit back and
can actually sit down

00:33:30.651 --> 00:33:31.650
and look at the numbers.

00:33:31.650 --> 00:33:33.240
Wikipedia has a
great table on this

00:33:33.240 --> 00:33:34.740
and we'll link it
in the show notes.

00:33:34.740 --> 00:33:39.540
But for example, so
five nines per year

00:33:39.540 --> 00:33:42.110
gives you 5 minutes
and 26 seconds,

00:33:42.110 --> 00:33:45.202
or 5.26 minutes of downtime.

00:33:45.202 --> 00:33:46.410
That's sort of what it means.

00:33:46.410 --> 00:33:52.230
Or essentially 25 seconds per
month, which is not a lot.

00:33:52.230 --> 00:33:54.300
And so the question
you have to ask

00:33:54.300 --> 00:33:57.637
yourself is what is the
SLA of your application.

00:33:57.637 --> 00:33:59.220
Rokesh uses the term
mission critical.

00:33:59.220 --> 00:34:01.300
What does that mean?

00:34:01.300 --> 00:34:03.720
Does your application have
to be up 100% of the time?

00:34:03.720 --> 00:34:05.170
Some applications are
like that, and that's

00:34:05.170 --> 00:34:06.086
certainly fair enough.

00:34:06.086 --> 00:34:08.610
But can your application handle
five minutes of downtime,

00:34:08.610 --> 00:34:10.776
or could you put in say a
caching layer or something

00:34:10.776 --> 00:34:12.300
like that to
mitigate any downtime

00:34:12.300 --> 00:34:14.760
from your back-end systems or
allow for some functionality,

00:34:14.760 --> 00:34:15.790
but not all.

00:34:15.790 --> 00:34:17.581
And I think those are
the sort of questions

00:34:17.581 --> 00:34:19.915
that we need to be talking
about when we build software.

00:34:19.915 --> 00:34:21.706
We can't just be sort
of hand-wavy and say,

00:34:21.706 --> 00:34:24.030
it must be up all the time
because otherwise bad things

00:34:24.030 --> 00:34:24.529
happen.

00:34:24.529 --> 00:34:26.370
We have to think
back and say software

00:34:26.370 --> 00:34:29.040
fails Come from the perspective
that software fails.

00:34:29.040 --> 00:34:31.520
Let's go back and talk to John
Wilkes again because we can.

00:34:31.520 --> 00:34:33.929
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I was
exactly thinking about that.

00:34:33.929 --> 00:34:37.920
The episode that we did with him
where basically, Borg and also

00:34:37.920 --> 00:34:42.630
Kubernetes are built with the
whole idea of, software fails,

00:34:42.630 --> 00:34:43.949
everything fails.

00:34:43.949 --> 00:34:47.529
Let's build something that
is kind of reliable on top

00:34:47.529 --> 00:34:48.570
of something that is not.

00:34:48.570 --> 00:34:51.087
MARK MANDEL: Those are the
questions that should be asked.

00:34:51.087 --> 00:34:52.170
That's pretty interesting.

00:34:52.170 --> 00:34:53.159
When you actually
break it down though,

00:34:53.159 --> 00:34:54.617
you look at the
nines availability,

00:34:54.617 --> 00:34:56.360
like, two nines, so 99%.

00:34:56.360 --> 00:34:59.292
That's 3.65 days per year.

00:34:59.292 --> 00:35:01.000
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That
starts to be a lot.

00:35:01.000 --> 00:35:01.595
I mean--

00:35:01.595 --> 00:35:03.470
MARK MANDEL: It does,
but that sort of like--

00:35:03.470 --> 00:35:05.053
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I
guess that I am not

00:35:05.053 --> 00:35:06.710
available that much as a person.

00:35:06.710 --> 00:35:10.330
MARK MANDEL: That's
seven hours a month.

00:35:10.330 --> 00:35:13.165
In some cases that could
actually be an OK SLA depending

00:35:13.165 --> 00:35:14.290
on what it is you're doing.

00:35:14.290 --> 00:35:17.620
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:35:17.620 --> 00:35:21.100
On the most available part,
if you get nine nines,

00:35:21.100 --> 00:35:25.860
you get 31 milliseconds
per year of off time.

00:35:25.860 --> 00:35:29.980
So basically, I wonder if
anyone has enough probes,

00:35:29.980 --> 00:35:31.402
like, so often to--

00:35:31.402 --> 00:35:32.610
MARK MANDEL: To guarantee it.

00:35:32.610 --> 00:35:34.240
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, like actually

00:35:34.240 --> 00:35:37.510
drop one of the probes, so
it's like 31 milliseconds off.

00:35:37.510 --> 00:35:38.110
That is hard.

00:35:38.110 --> 00:35:41.440
MARK MANDEL: I feel like
you would drop TCP packets.

00:35:41.440 --> 00:35:44.174
And if you had to check it
some packet on that IP stream

00:35:44.174 --> 00:35:46.090
would end up disappearing
and you're like, oh,

00:35:46.090 --> 00:35:46.710
that's our SLA gone.

00:35:46.710 --> 00:35:49.126
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah I get
that nine nines is basically--

00:35:49.126 --> 00:35:50.887
nine nines and 100
are basically there.

00:35:50.887 --> 00:35:52.720
They're basically the
same thing and they're

00:35:52.720 --> 00:35:54.730
both pretty much unachievable.

00:35:54.730 --> 00:35:58.020
If you have a service that
offers nine nines please let

00:35:58.020 --> 00:35:58.520
us know.

00:35:58.520 --> 00:35:59.650
We'd like to interview you.

00:35:59.650 --> 00:36:02.920
I think that is a very
interesting set of requirements

00:36:02.920 --> 00:36:04.830
to achieve that availability.

00:36:04.830 --> 00:36:06.100
We'd love to talk to you.

00:36:06.100 --> 00:36:08.680
MARK MANDEL: And find
out how you achieved it.

00:36:08.680 --> 00:36:10.892
So yeah, to Rokesh,
I think what is

00:36:10.892 --> 00:36:12.100
the requirements of your app?

00:36:12.100 --> 00:36:13.090
What are your SLAs?

00:36:13.090 --> 00:36:14.700
What do you need
to be able to do?

00:36:14.700 --> 00:36:17.470
And what sort of failure
rate can your stuff require?

00:36:17.470 --> 00:36:19.180
And that will tell
you exactly what

00:36:19.180 --> 00:36:21.460
systems you can build on
top of and also how quickly

00:36:21.460 --> 00:36:23.020
and how majorly
you can change them

00:36:23.020 --> 00:36:24.576
while they're running as well.

00:36:24.576 --> 00:36:26.200
